1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
Remember the bottle that gave no change in

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:03,320
all three tests?

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,640
It could be cyclohexane, or it could be

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:11,920
2-methylpropan-2-ol, a tertiary alcohol that leaves dichromate orange.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,920
But one of them has an O H

6
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:15,600
group and the other doesn't.

7
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,880
An infrared spectrum shows bonds directly, so it

8
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:20,160
can settle this quickly.

9
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:24,646
Every covalent bond vibrates, a bit like two

10
00:00:24,807 --> 00:00:26,406
balls joined by a spring.

11
00:00:26,887 --> 00:00:29,926
The bond stretches and squashes, and it can

12
00:00:30,007 --> 00:00:30,726
bend.

13
00:00:31,047 --> 00:00:33,206
Each kind of bond has its own natural

14
00:00:33,287 --> 00:00:34,646
rate of vibration.

15
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:37,446
Shine infrared light through a sample, and a

16
00:00:37,527 --> 00:00:40,486
bond absorbs the infrared that matches its rate.

17
00:00:40,807 --> 00:00:43,046
That light doesn't come out the other side,

18
00:00:43,127 --> 00:00:44,966
so the instrument records a dip.

19
00:00:46,167 --> 00:00:49,126
Up the side is transmittance, the percentage of

20
00:00:49,207 --> 00:00:50,406
light that gets through.

21
00:00:50,887 --> 00:00:53,366
An absorption shows up as a dip pointing

22
00:00:53,447 --> 00:00:53,926
down.

23
00:00:54,407 --> 00:00:57,846
Along the bottom is wavenumber, in reciprocal centimetres.

24
00:00:58,327 --> 00:01:00,006
It's the number of waves that fit in

25
00:01:00,087 --> 00:01:03,206
one centimetre, and a higher wavenumber means more

26
00:01:03,287 --> 00:01:03,766
energy.

27
00:01:04,407 --> 00:01:07,046
Notice that the scale runs backwards, from four

28
00:01:07,127 --> 00:01:09,366
thousand on the left to about five hundred

29
00:01:09,447 --> 00:01:10,166
on the right.

30
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:12,646
Think of the spring again.

31
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:15,366
The atoms are the balls, and the bond

32
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:16,526
is the spring.

33
00:01:16,807 --> 00:01:20,166
A stiffer spring vibrates faster, so a stronger

34
00:01:20,247 --> 00:01:22,486
bond absorbs at a higher wavenumber.

35
00:01:23,047 --> 00:01:25,526
So a C equals O double bond sits

36
00:01:25,607 --> 00:01:28,486
near seventeen hundred, above a C O single

37
00:01:28,567 --> 00:01:29,206
bond.

38
00:01:29,527 --> 00:01:31,726
Lighter balls vibrate faster too.

39
00:01:32,007 --> 00:01:34,806
Hydrogen is the lightest atom, so bonds to

40
00:01:34,887 --> 00:01:37,286
hydrogen sit at the high end, near three

41
00:01:37,447 --> 00:01:38,726
thousand and above.

42
00:01:39,047 --> 00:01:41,526
The analogy has a limit: a spring can

43
00:01:41,607 --> 00:01:44,406
vibrate with any energy, but a bond only

44
00:01:44,527 --> 00:01:46,166
absorbs particular ones.

45
00:01:47,367 --> 00:01:49,526
You don't need to memorise where each bond

46
00:01:49,647 --> 00:01:52,766
absorbs, because the data sheet gives the ranges.

47
00:01:53,047 --> 00:01:54,966
Your job is to match a dip to

48
00:01:55,007 --> 00:01:57,286
a range, and then use the shape and

49
00:01:57,367 --> 00:01:58,966
the combination of dips.

50
00:01:59,287 --> 00:02:01,766
Four rows do most of the work: O

51
00:02:01,927 --> 00:02:05,286
H, C H, C equals O and N

52
00:02:05,367 --> 00:02:07,286
H, with help from C O.

53
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:09,860
Start with C H.

54
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:13,780
Almost every organic molecule has C H bonds,

55
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:17,060
so almost every spectrum has sharp dips just

56
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:18,740
below three thousand.

57
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:22,180
Here's cyclohexane: it shows strong C H dips

58
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:24,980
and very little else above fifteen hundred.

59
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:27,380
So it rarely decides anything.

60
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,220
Note it, and look past it.

61
00:02:30,434 --> 00:02:32,833
Now the O H in an alcohol.

62
00:02:33,154 --> 00:02:36,273
Alcohol molecules hydrogen bond to each other, and

63
00:02:36,434 --> 00:02:38,913
each hydrogen bond weakens its O H by

64
00:02:38,994 --> 00:02:40,433
a slightly different amount.

65
00:02:40,994 --> 00:02:42,993
So the O H bonds absorb over a

66
00:02:43,074 --> 00:02:46,113
spread of wavenumbers, and you get one broad,

67
00:02:46,354 --> 00:02:49,473
rounded band between about thirty-two hundred and thirty-five

68
00:02:49,554 --> 00:02:50,433
hundred.

69
00:02:50,514 --> 00:02:53,553
In ethanol it's centred near thirty-three forty.

70
00:02:54,767 --> 00:02:57,966
A carboxylic acid has an O H too.

71
00:02:58,287 --> 00:03:01,246
Acid molecules pair up, held by two strong

72
00:03:01,327 --> 00:03:02,566
hydrogen bonds.

73
00:03:02,847 --> 00:03:05,566
That weakens each O H even more, and

74
00:03:05,647 --> 00:03:08,206
over a wider range, so the band sits

75
00:03:08,367 --> 00:03:11,246
lower and is very broad, from about twenty-five

76
00:03:11,327 --> 00:03:14,286
hundred to three thousand, running right over the

77
00:03:14,367 --> 00:03:15,486
C H dips.

78
00:03:15,807 --> 00:03:18,366
An acid also brings a strong C equals

79
00:03:18,607 --> 00:03:20,766
O near seventeen ten.

80
00:03:20,847 --> 00:03:22,926
So a very broad O H plus a

81
00:03:23,007 --> 00:03:25,326
C equals O points to an acid.

82
00:03:26,534 --> 00:03:28,453
The C equals O dip is one of

83
00:03:28,534 --> 00:03:31,973
the easiest to spot: strong and sharp, between

84
00:03:32,054 --> 00:03:34,533
sixteen eighty and seventeen fifty.

85
00:03:34,654 --> 00:03:37,653
Propanone, a ketone, shows it at about seventeen

86
00:03:37,814 --> 00:03:40,293
fifteen, with no O H band at all.

87
00:03:40,774 --> 00:03:44,453
But ketones, aldehydes, esters and acids all have

88
00:03:44,494 --> 00:03:45,493
a C equals O.

89
00:03:45,974 --> 00:03:47,893
So this dip tells you there's a carbonyl

90
00:03:47,974 --> 00:03:49,653
group, not which kind.

91
00:03:50,054 --> 00:03:52,213
The other dips, and the formula, help you

92
00:03:52,334 --> 00:03:52,933
decide.

93
00:03:54,134 --> 00:03:57,653
Primary and secondary amines have N H bonds,

94
00:03:57,734 --> 00:04:01,253
which absorb from thirty-three hundred to thirty-five hundred,

95
00:04:01,334 --> 00:04:04,373
overlapping the alcohol O H range.

96
00:04:04,414 --> 00:04:06,533
But an N H dip is narrower and

97
00:04:06,614 --> 00:04:08,853
weaker than an O H band.

98
00:04:08,974 --> 00:04:12,293
Propan-1-amine, with an N H two group, shows

99
00:04:12,374 --> 00:04:13,813
two N H bands.

100
00:04:15,034 --> 00:04:18,473
Below about fifteen hundred, the spectrum gets crowded.

101
00:04:19,034 --> 00:04:22,073
Whole groups of atoms vibrate together, and every

102
00:04:22,154 --> 00:04:25,033
molecule makes its own pattern, so it's called

103
00:04:25,074 --> 00:04:26,553
the fingerprint region.

104
00:04:26,874 --> 00:04:30,313
Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol have the same kinds of

105
00:04:30,394 --> 00:04:32,873
bond, so they show the same bands above

106
00:04:32,954 --> 00:04:35,953
fifteen hundred, but their fingerprints differ.

107
00:04:36,314 --> 00:04:38,313
Use it to match a known spectrum, and

108
00:04:38,394 --> 00:04:40,233
to pick out a strong C O band

109
00:04:40,434 --> 00:04:42,713
between one thousand and thirteen hundred.

110
00:04:43,934 --> 00:04:45,453
So here's a routine.

111
00:04:45,694 --> 00:04:46,973
Scan from the left.

112
00:04:47,214 --> 00:04:49,693
Is there a broad O H around thirty-three

113
00:04:49,854 --> 00:04:50,573
hundred?

114
00:04:50,614 --> 00:04:53,133
A very broad one, from twenty-five hundred to

115
00:04:53,214 --> 00:04:54,173
three thousand?

116
00:04:54,214 --> 00:04:57,373
A strong C equals O near seventeen hundred?

117
00:04:57,414 --> 00:04:59,773
A strong C O between one thousand and

118
00:04:59,854 --> 00:05:00,973
thirteen hundred?

119
00:05:01,014 --> 00:05:03,133
And a missing band is evidence too.

120
00:05:03,374 --> 00:05:06,413
No dip between sixteen eighty and seventeen fifty

121
00:05:06,654 --> 00:05:09,133
rules out the carbonyl candidates on your list.

122
00:05:10,334 --> 00:05:11,573
Back to the bottle.

123
00:05:11,854 --> 00:05:14,813
Its spectrum has a broad, rounded band centred

124
00:05:14,934 --> 00:05:16,653
near thirty-three seventy.

125
00:05:16,734 --> 00:05:18,253
That's an alcohol O H.

126
00:05:18,734 --> 00:05:20,933
There's no C equals O dip, and there's

127
00:05:20,974 --> 00:05:23,213
a strong band near twelve hundred, in the

128
00:05:23,374 --> 00:05:24,533
C O range.

129
00:05:24,894 --> 00:05:27,613
Cyclohexane has no O H and no C

130
00:05:27,694 --> 00:05:30,013
O, so it can't give these dips.

131
00:05:30,334 --> 00:05:32,973
The bottle holds 2-methylpropan-2-ol.

132
00:05:33,454 --> 00:05:35,293
The spectrum found the O H that the

133
00:05:35,374 --> 00:05:36,653
tests couldn't see.

134
00:05:37,867 --> 00:05:41,146
Here's a picture to remember: beard plus sword

135
00:05:41,307 --> 00:05:42,426
means acid.

136
00:05:42,907 --> 00:05:45,626
The very broad acid O H hangs down

137
00:05:45,707 --> 00:05:48,186
like a beard, and the sharp C equals

138
00:05:48,347 --> 00:05:49,706
O is the sword.

139
00:05:50,187 --> 00:05:52,666
A rounded O H with no sword points

140
00:05:52,747 --> 00:05:53,786
to an alcohol.

141
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:55,560
Your turn.

142
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:59,560
Compound X is either butanoic acid or ethyl

143
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:00,360
ethanoate.

144
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:03,000
Use the spectrum and the band values on

145
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:05,960
the card to identify X, and justify your

146
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:06,440
answer.

147
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:08,200
Pause here to answer.

148
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:21,480
Both candidates have a C equals O, so

149
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:24,040
the dip at seventeen forty doesn't decide.

150
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:26,120
The missing band does.

151
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:28,600
An acid would show a very broad O

152
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:31,800
H from twenty-five hundred to three thousand, and

153
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:33,320
X has none.

154
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:35,880
The C O dips at twelve forty and

155
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,480
ten fifty fit an ester.

156
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,360
So X is ethyl ethanoate.

157
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:42,520
Calling it an acid because it has C

158
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:45,400
equals O and C O is the slip:

159
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:47,240
no beard, no acid.

160
00:06:48,467 --> 00:06:49,106
Last one.

161
00:06:49,667 --> 00:06:52,226
Liquid Z is either an alcohol or an

162
00:06:52,307 --> 00:06:56,226
aldehyde, and it turns acidified dichromate green.

163
00:06:56,547 --> 00:06:58,626
Use the spectrum on the card to identify

164
00:06:58,707 --> 00:07:01,026
the type of compound, and explain why the

165
00:07:01,107 --> 00:07:03,586
dichromate result alone couldn't.

166
00:07:03,907 --> 00:07:05,186
Pause here to answer.

167
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:19,120
Green only shows something was oxidised, and aldehydes

168
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,360
do that as well as primary and secondary

169
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:22,640
alcohols.

170
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:25,920
Nothing between sixteen eighty and seventeen fifty means

171
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:29,280
no C equals O, so Z isn't an

172
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:30,000
aldehyde.

173
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:32,960
The broad band at thirty-three thirty is an

174
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:34,400
alcohol O H.

175
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:37,680
So Z is a primary or secondary alcohol.

176
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,640
Calling the dips near twenty-nine sixty the O

177
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,600
H is the trap: they're C H.

178
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:48,000
So, when you meet an infrared spectrum, scan

179
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:49,480
from left to right.

180
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:52,240
Check the O H region and its shape,

181
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:55,200
then the C equals O, then the C

182
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:55,520
O.

183
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:58,400
Name each bond from the data sheet, say

184
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:00,880
what's missing, and combine it with the formula

185
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:02,240
and the mass.

186
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:05,520
Next, NMR, which shows how the carbon and

187
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:07,440
hydrogen atoms are arranged.

